Resumen

Introduced: the commit added the operator alert webhook feature without backend URL validation or network egress restrictions.

TASK-0057 adds complaint alert webhooks under tenant notification settings. The tenant settings PATCH endpoint blindly accepts arbitrary `notification_settings` JSON, `normalize_alert_channels` keeps any non-empty `webhook_url` string, and the alert worker later calls `httpx.AsyncClient.post(url, ...)` directly from the backend. There is no enforcement of HTTPS, no allowlist, no DNS/IP resolution checks, and no blocking of localhost, RFC1918, link-local, or cloud metadata addresses. A malicious tenant admin can set `complaint_alert_channels.webhook_url` to an internal URL such as `http://127.0.0.1:...`, `http://169.254.169.254/...`, or another private service, then trigger a negative-feedback alert and cause the scheduler/alerts worker to send a server-side POST from the application network. This is a new SSRF sink and can be used for blind internal service interaction, potential state-changing requests to internal HTTP services, egress probing, and disclosure of alert payload data to attacker-controlled endpoints.

Validación

 Confirm tenant-admin settings update accepts and persists arbitrary notification_settings including complaint_alert_channels.webhook_url without backend URL validation.
 Confirm normalize_alert_channels preserves arbitrary non-empty webhook strings, including loopback/private-style HTTP URLs.
 Confirm alert enqueue copies the configured webhook URL into app.operator_alerts.payload.channels when a negative-feedback/complaint alert is created.
 Confirm the dispatcher/scheduler path sends from backend code to the configured URL using real HTTP client code and no URL/IP/redirect safety gate.
 Dynamically demonstrate a POST from the application process to 127.0.0.1 and capture the request and payload; corroborate with a debugger stack trace at the sink.
