Análisis de la ruta de ataque

Kept the original high severity. The evidence supports a real, in-scope SSRF: tenant-admin-controlled notification_settings are persisted without validation, the webhook_url is preserved unchanged, queued into operator_alerts, and later POSTed by httpx from the scheduler/alerts worker running in the backend environment. The validation evidence further proves loopback reachability. Severity is high because this crosses a trust boundary into server-side network egress and can target private/metadata/internal services. It is not raised to critical because exploitation requires tenant-admin privileges and the demonstrated sink is blind POST-only without proven response exfiltration, cloud credential theft, code execution, or tenant-wide/platform-wide compromise.

Ruta
Authenticated tenant admin --PATCHes tenant settings--> Tenant settings PATCH accepts notification_settings --persists arbitrary JSON--> Unvalidated complaint_alert_channels.webhook_url --copied into alert channels--> Negative feedback enqueues app.operator_alerts row --pending alert is polled--> Scheduler/alerts worker in backend support mode --dispatches webhook--> httpx POST to attacker-selected URL --server-side network request--> Internal service, loopback, RFC1918, link-local, or metadata endpoint
The finding is real and reachable. The tenant-admin settings route is part of the main /v1 FastAPI API and accepts notification_settings in an arbitrary dict payload, then stores it as JSONB without URL validation. normalize_alert_channels only strips any string webhook_url. When negative feedback is processed, enqueue_operator_alert copies those channels into a pending operator_alerts row. The scheduler is a deployed backend service and processes pending alerts in support mode; dispatch_operator_alert passes channels['webhook_url'] to _send_webhook_channel, which directly calls httpx.AsyncClient.post(url, ...). Repository evidence shows API/admin ports are exposed in docker-compose and internal services such as postgres, redis, and minio exist on the deployment network. The validation PoC reportedly used the real enqueue and dispatch functions and proved a backend POST to 127.0.0.1. Severity remains high, not critical: exploitation requires tenant-admin privileges and the SSRF is blind POST-only with no response body returned to the attacker, but it crosses the tenant/application trust boundary into backend network egress and can target loopback/private/link-local/metadata services.

Probabilidad
High - Likelihood is medium: the exploit path is straightforward after obtaining tenant-admin access and uses normal product features, and validation demonstrated a loopback POST through real enqueue/dispatch functions. However, it is not public/unauthenticated; it requires an authenticated tenant admin and an alert-triggering condition, and actual internal impact depends on deployment egress and reachable services. | Remote network vector
Impacto
High - Impact is high because a tenant admin can cause a trusted backend worker to initiate HTTP POSTs to arbitrary internal, loopback, link-local, or metadata-network URLs. This crosses the tenant-to-backend-network boundary and can enable blind internal service interaction, egress probing, state-changing POSTs against unauthenticated internal HTTP services, and attempted metadata access. It is not rated critical because the route requires tenant-admin privileges, the sink uses POST and does not return response bodies to the attacker, and direct credential disclosure or code execution is not proven from repository artifacts.
